Result: Partial. The ingest and streaming code is covered, but only part of `cli.py` and none of `config.py`. Line numbers for every file except `cli.py` come from counting the unnumbered read output, so each may be off by 1–2. The `cli.py` numbers come from grep.

Findings:
- `src/eurostream/streaming.py:269` — High, logic, ingest. `commit()` runs per record, but the scorer state is never checkpointed; I found no `checkpoint(`/`restore(` call in the `cli.py` grep. A restart with a committed offset loses the velocity counts and z-score history, so VELOCITY and AMOUNT_ZSCORE alerts are silently missed. Spark would need `applyInPandasWithState` or `transformWithState` with a checkpoint.
- `src/eurostream/streaming.py:261` — High, logic, ingest. Alerts are produced to `fraud_alerts` and the input offset is committed on the next line (269). On Kafka the produce is async and `poll(0)` is the only delivery handling (`bus/kafka.py:98`). A crash or delivery failure loses alerts permanently. The `checkpoint` docstring claims "exactly-once recovery" (line 163), but it is not wired in.
- `src/eurostream/bus/kafka.py:83` — High, data_quality, ingest. The delivery callback only logs errors (83–84). `produce` returns a fake `RecordMetadata(offset=0)` (99), so callers cannot detect failure or know the real offset.
- `src/eurostream/bus/kafka.py:157` — Medium, data_quality, ingest. Kafka poll errors are logged and return `None`. The streaming loop treats `None` as idle and stops after 5 idle polls (`streaming.py:234`), so a broker error can silently end the run.
- `src/eurostream/streaming.py:249` — Medium, data_quality, ingest. Malformed or invalid payloads only increment `malformed_events` and are committed. There is no dead-letter queue, so bad data is dropped.
- `src/eurostream/streaming.py:241` — Medium, logic, ingest. `expire()` is called every 50 records with wall-clock `time.time()` (line 242). Windows are keyed on event `occurred_at`, so replay or backfill with `earliest` would evict state wrongly. There is no watermark or late-data handling.
- `src/eurostream/streaming.py:96` — Medium, logic, ingest. VELOCITY fires only when `count == threshold + 1`, once per window. Windows are tumbling and per-process. Dedup by `event_id` is absent, so a redelivered payment inflates counts.
- `src/eurostream/streaming.py:67` — Low, logic, ingest. Thresholds use `x or settings.x`, so an explicit 0 silently falls back to the setting. Same pattern at `producers.py:90` (`amount or random`).
- `src/eurostream/streaming.py:242` — Medium, data_quality, ingest. Timestamps are `float` epoch seconds from the producer clock (`models.py:38`). Time is taken from `time.time()`/`time.time()` calls, with no timezone and no event-time source of truth (alert_ts at 103).
- `src/eurostream/producers.py:114` — Critical, security, ingest. Order events are serialised with `model_dump_json()` and carry `email` and `iban` in clear text on the bus topic `orders`. Payments carry `iban` (157) and clicks carry `ip_address` (130). All of these persist in `events.db`, so erasure must reach the bus log.
- `src/eurostream/bus/sqlite.py:20` — High, governance, store. The `messages` table is append-only with no delete or retention path in the code I read. `value` holds the clear-text PII, so erasure does not provably reach it.
- `src/eurostream/bus/sqlite.py:55` — Medium, concurrency, store. One shared `sqlite3` connection with `check_same_thread=False` is guarded by a process-local `threading.Lock`, and consumers reuse it (128). It is not safe across processes: `produce` and `stream` run as separate CLI processes on the same file.
- `src/eurostream/bus/sqlite.py:97` — Low, scope, ingest. Partition is hard-coded to 0 in both produce (97) and consumer (139). `poll` ignores its `timeout` (158). Ordering is single-partition only, whereas Kafka keyed by customer would spread across partitions.
- `src/eurostream/bus/kafka.py:112` — Medium, ops, ingest. The consumer default `auto_offset_reset="latest"` differs from the CLI's `earliest` usage (`cli.py:158`). `enable.auto.commit` is False (126) with a synchronous commit (203), which is correct but is one commit per record.
- `src/eurostream/bus/kafka.py:24` — Low, security, ingest. Credentials are accepted embedded in a bootstrap URI and then stripped. The bootstrap, username and password come from `Settings` (config not read). Whether any default password is hard-coded is unverified.
- `src/eurostream/models.py:83` — Low, security, ingest. The default `requested_by="dsar@eurocart.eu"` is hard-coded. The `eurocart` brand name is a stale artefact.
- `src/eurostream/models.py:41` — Low, data_quality, ingest. `partition_key()` returns `event_id`, but every producer keys by `customer_id` (`producers.py:113`). The contract and the practice disagree.
- `src/eurostream/producers.py:59` — Low, governance, ingest. `marketing_consent` defaults to `settings.consent_default`, and the generator is synthetic, so the real source ingest is out of scope.
- `src/eurostream/cli.py:47` — Medium, ops, ingest. Backend selection is `Kafka` vs `open_bus(settings.data_dir / "events.db")` in `_fresh()` (47–58). I did not read lines 40–60 in full, so whether Kafka failure falls back silently to SQLite is unconfirmed.

